WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.256
This final video turns the senior responsibilities in both postings into concrete habits, and shows how to make your impact visible.

00:00:08.456 --> 00:00:13.181
A junior engineer fixes a bug. A senior engineer fixes the class of bug.

00:00:13.431 --> 00:00:16.798
A staff engineer makes sure the whole team never ships it again.

00:00:17.048 --> 00:00:19.289
Let's turn that into weekly habits.

00:00:20.339 --> 00:00:22.940
A senior engineer fixes the hard bug.

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:27.740
A staff engineer makes sure the whole team stops creating that kind of bug.

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:31.704
They do it by changing the design, the tools, or the process.

00:00:32.904 --> 00:00:33.601
Words.

00:00:34.001 --> 00:00:40.502
A design doc covers context, options, trade offs, the decision, rollout, and a test plan.

00:00:40.902 --> 00:00:44.861
Blast radius is how many products or users a change can affect.

00:00:45.261 --> 00:00:48.675
A brag doc is your own running log of impact, with evidence.

00:00:49.075 --> 00:00:53.812
And lean means small batches, fast feedback, and less waiting and rework.

00:00:55.012 --> 00:00:57.798
The postings' verbs, turned into habits.

00:00:58.198 --> 00:01:04.688
Lead C and C plus plus: own one subsystem end to end, for example the U V C gadget path.

00:01:05.088 --> 00:01:17.963
UVC: USB Video Class: the standard "I am a webcam" language every computer already understands. Like: A universal power plug for cameras: plug in anywhere and it just works, no driver install.

00:01:18.263 --> 00:01:23.964
Define standards: write a USB descriptor and composite rules document, and get it adopted.

00:01:24.364 --> 00:01:27.603
Descriptor: A small table of facts the device reports about itself: who made it, what it can do. Like: A business card plus resume the device hands over when it is first plugged in.

00:01:27.853 --> 00:01:34.668
Composite device: One USB device that offers several functions at once, like camera plus mic plus buttons. Like: A multi-tool: one handle, several blades.

00:01:34.918 --> 00:01:39.365
Troubleshoot: be the person who brings the analyzer trace and the root cause write up.

00:01:39.765 --> 00:01:44.339
Testing strategy: land unit, QEMU and hardware jobs for your area.

00:01:44.739 --> 00:01:53.052
QEMU: A program that emulates a whole computer, so you can test kernels without real hardware. Like: A flight simulator for software.

00:01:53.352 --> 00:01:57.996
Mentor: run a monthly deep dive, and write code reviews that teach.

00:01:58.396 --> 00:02:03.841
Agile and lean: measure one thing, like build time or flaky test rate, and improve it.

00:02:05.041 --> 00:02:07.874
Here's how one bug becomes staff level impact.

00:02:08.274 --> 00:02:09.412
Fix the bug.

00:02:09.812 --> 00:02:11.530
Write a short root cause note.

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:15.111
Add a regression test, so it never comes back here.

00:02:15.511 --> 00:02:19.621
Add a checklist item or CI rule, so it never happens anywhere.

00:02:20.021 --> 00:02:32.084
CI / HIL: Continuous integration runs tests on every change; hardware-in-the-loop (HIL) runs them on real boards. Like: A quality inspector at every station of the assembly line, some with real parts on the bench.

00:02:32.384 --> 00:02:33.754
Teach it in a deep dive.

00:02:34.154 --> 00:02:38.159
And change the design, so this class of bug becomes impossible.

00:02:39.359 --> 00:02:40.299
Showing impact.

00:02:40.699 --> 00:02:51.880
Use numbers. For example: frame drops reduced from two point one percent to zero point zero five, or enumeration failures down eighty percent. These are example targets, not facts.

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:59.525
Enumeration: The first conversation after plug-in where the laptop learns what the device is and gives it an address. Like: A new employee's first morning: show ID, get a badge number, hand in a resume, get assigned a desk.

00:02:59.775 --> 00:03:05.080
Keep a weekly brag doc: the problem, what you did, the result, and who benefited.

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:08.534
Send a short monthly update tied to your manager's goals.

00:03:08.934 --> 00:03:14.135
Run clear, reproducible vendor escalations. That visibility is staff level work.

00:03:14.535 --> 00:03:17.693
And when allowed, send a few upstream kernel patches.

00:03:18.093 --> 00:03:24.815
Kernel: The core of the operating system that controls memory, devices and which program runs. Like: The building manager who owns every key and decides who uses which room.

00:03:25.865 --> 00:03:32.460
Question for you: You fixed a tricky USB descriptor bug. What would turn that fix into senior-level impact?

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:47.384
Answer: Add a test that catches it automatically, write the rule into the team's descriptor checklist, and share the debugging story in a short team session.

00:03:48.484 --> 00:03:50.202
Let's close with the plan.

00:03:50.602 --> 00:03:56.338
Days one to thirty: learn the house. Build, flash, debug, and map the stack.

00:03:56.738 --> 00:04:01.231
Days thirty one to sixty: own a slice, like the USB device path.

00:04:01.631 --> 00:04:06.635
Days sixty one to ninety: a design doc, a deep dive, and agreed goals.

00:04:07.035 --> 00:04:09.926
Months four to twelve: multiply the team.

00:04:10.326 --> 00:04:14.552
The full plan is in the learning plan file. Good luck. You've got this.
